@@ -0,0 +1,233 @@
+# OP_CHECKSLHDSA128S: an experimental SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s check in P2MR leaves
+
+This build adds one opcode, `OP_CHECKSLHDSA128S`, that verifies an SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s signature
+([FIPS 205](https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.FIPS.205)) inside a P2MR script leaf (see
+[p2mr.md](p2mr.md)). It is an experiment for the Ark-0 test signet, part M4-S of its M4 series.
+It is not part of BIP 360, which defines no post-quantum signature check, it is not the
+"Algorithm Agility" proposal or any other draft's opcode, and it is not proposed for mainnet.
+
+What it shows is narrow: a node running these rules refuses, at the block level, any spend
+through the leaf template SLH1 below that lacks a valid SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s signature. Other
+scripts can use the opcode in ways that need no signature at all (sixteen empty signature chunks
+push false, which `OP_NOT` turns into true), so only the template is relayed, and only it should
+be used. SLH-DSA does not bind the message (FIPS 205 section 11): the holder of a key can make
+one signature that is valid for two messages, so a signature must never be taken as a commitment
+to the message it signs.
+
+**On Ark-0 from height 32000.** The Ark-0 signet applies these rules from block 32000
+(`ARK0_SLHDSA128S_HEIGHT`, see [Activation](#activation)); the validation weight of a check is
+5,000 (see [Validation weight](#validation-weight)). Both are fixed and do not change.
+
+## Where the opcode executes
+
+`OP_CHECKSLHDSA128S` is byte `0xf1`, which BIP 342 defines as `OP_SUCCESS241`. The byte executes
+as `OP_CHECKSLHDSA128S` only when all of these hold:
+
+- the script is executed as tapscript (`SigVersion::TAPSCRIPT`);
+- it is a P2MR leaf that is executed: leaf version `0xc0`, or `0xe0` once it executes
+  ([p2mr-tripwire.md](p2mr-tripwire.md)), at depth one or more (a leaf at depth zero is not
+  executed at all);
+- `SCRIPT_VERIFY_P2MR_SLHDSA128S` applies, i.e. the rules are active for the block (see
+  [Activation](#activation)).
+
+Then the pre-scan of the leaf no longer treats `0xf1` as an `OP_SUCCESSx`; any other
+`OP_SUCCESSx` in the same leaf still makes the whole script succeed.
+
+Everywhere else the byte keeps its current meaning: a bad opcode in legacy and witness v0
+scripts, and `OP_SUCCESS241` in P2TR tapscript leaves and in P2MR leaves before activation.
+**A leaf using the opcode therefore protects nothing in a P2TR output, or in a P2MR output spent
+before activation: an inactive SLH-DSA leaf is not a safe fallback.**
+
+Neither the disabled elliptic curve checks of leaf version `0xe0`
+([p2mr-tripwire.md](p2mr-tripwire.md)) nor anything else turns this opcode off.
+
+## Encoding
+
+No stack element may exceed 520 bytes, and this opcode does not change that. The parameter set
+has n = 16, h = 63, d = 7, h' = 9, a = 12, k = 14 and lg_w = 4, so the signature is
+(1 + 182 + 63 + 245) x 16 = 7,856 bytes (7,857 with an explicit hash type), split into chunks of
+fixed sizes so that each signature has exactly one encoding:
+
+- the public key: 32 bytes, PK.seed followed by PK.root, pushed by the leaf itself;
+- the signature: sixteen witness elements, fifteen of 520 bytes and one of 56 (57 when it ends in
+  a hash type).
+
+The one template, **SLH1**, is the leaf `20 <32-byte public key> f1`, 34 bytes:
+
+    leaf:     <pk> OP_CHECKSLHDSA128S
+    witness:  <sig[0]:520> ... <sig[14]:520> <sig[15]:56 or 57> <leaf> <control block>
+
+A single-signature spend at depth one has a witness of 7,972 bytes (7,973 with a hash type), and
+so a validation weight budget of 8,022.
+
+## Execution
+
+The opcode takes seventeen stack elements: the public key on top, then `sig[15]` down to
+`sig[0]`. The checks run in this order, which follows `OP_CHECKMLDSA44`, which follows BIP 342's
+`OP_CHECKSIG`:
+
+1. at least seventeen elements on the stack, or `SCRIPT_ERR_INVALID_STACK_OPERATION`;
+2. the key and the sixteen chunks are taken;
+3. if any chunk is non-empty, `VALIDATION_WEIGHT_PER_SLHDSA128S_CHECK` is taken from the input's
+   budget, and a negative budget fails the script (`SCRIPT_ERR_TAPSCRIPT_VALIDATION_WEIGHT`);
+4. the public key must be exactly 32 bytes (`SCRIPT_ERR_SLHDSA128S_PUBKEY_SIZE`), even when the
+   signature is empty; there is no upgradable key type;
+5. sixteen empty chunks are an empty signature: the opcode pushes an empty element (false) and
+   execution goes on;
+6. otherwise the chunks must be `520 x 15, 56` or `520 x 15, 57` (`SCRIPT_ERR_SLHDSA128S_SIG_SIZE`).
+   A chunk over 520 bytes never gets here: the initial stack check refuses it first
+   (`SCRIPT_ERR_PUSH_SIZE`);
+7. a 57th byte of the last chunk is an explicit hash type with the rules of BIP 341: `0x00` written
+   out, any value but `0x01` to `0x03` and `0x81` to `0x83`, and `SIGHASH_SINGLE` without an output
+   of the same index fail (`SCRIPT_ERR_SLHDSA128S_SIG_HASHTYPE`);
+8. the message is the BIP 341 signature message of a tapscript spend with **key_version 2**
+   (`SignatureHashSchnorr`), which commits to the annex, the position of the last executed
+   `OP_CODESEPARATOR` and the tapleaf hash, leaf version included. key_version is 0 for BIP 342
+   signatures and 1 for `OP_CHECKMLDSA44`, so no signature message of one is that of another;
+9. the signature is checked with `slh_verify` of FIPS 205, the external interface of pure
+   SLH-DSA, with the context string `P2MR/SLHDSA128S/EXP0` (20 bytes). It is not HashSLH-DSA and
+   not the internal interface. An invalid signature fails the script
+   (`SCRIPT_ERR_SLHDSA128S_SIG`): it does not push false, so `OP_NOT` cannot turn it around;
+10. a valid signature pushes `0x01`.
+
+The order only decides which error is reported, not whether the script passes.
+
+The caching signature checker of block and mempool validation caches valid checks in the
+signature cache under their own salted hasher, padded with `H`, apart from the ECDSA (`E`),
+Schnorr (`S`) and ML-DSA-44 (`M`) entries. `DeferringSignatureChecker` passes the check through.
+
+## Verification library
+
+The verifier is [slhdsa-c](https://github.com/pq-code-package/slhdsa-c) at commit
+`a0fc1ff253930060d0246aebca06c2538eb92b88`, the commit liboqs 0.15.0 uses, in
+`src/crypto/slhdsa128s/` with its own portable C SHA-256 (no SHA extensions, the same code on
+every machine). `src/crypto/slhdsa128s/README.md` lists every file taken and its SHA-256. The
+upstream project calls the library work in progress, so it is checked against the NIST ACVP
+vectors and another implementation, and fuzzed.
+
+`SLHDSA128SVerify` in `src/crypto/slhdsa128s.h` is the only entry point. It checks the lengths of
+the key (32), the message (32, a signature message), the signature (7,856) and the context string
+(at most 255) before it calls the library: the vendored `slh_verify` hashes the first 16 bytes of the signature before it checks
+the signature's length, so a shorter one must never reach it.
+
+## Validation weight
+
+Each check with a signature uses `VALIDATION_WEIGHT_PER_SLHDSA128S_CHECK` units of the input's
+budget, which BIP 342 sets to the witness size plus 50 and charges 50 per Schnorr check.
+
+**The value is 5,000, final.** It never changes on a network once a build carrying it has been
+published. It was set from these rules:
+
+- the single-check condition is `C >= 1.25 x 50 x p99(worst-case SLH-DSA check) / median(Schnorr
+  check)`, both over the whole path of a check, measured with a SHA-256 that uses no SHA
+  extensions;
+- the worst case is that of the verification itself: every WOTS+ chain of every layer at its
+  longest (510 steps), 3,894 SHA-256 compressions against about 2,194 on average. An honest
+  signature cannot be pushed there, but the weight is set for it so that it does not depend on
+  how much work an attacker spends grinding;
+- it must stay below 8,022, the budget of a single-signature spend. At 5,000 a single SLH1 spend
+  fits, and so does one check per signature in any script; checking one signature twice (with
+  `OP_PICK`, say) does not fit the budget of a single signature.
+
+Measurement (2026-10-15): on a cloud virtual machine (Intel Xeon at 2.8 GHz, no SHA extensions,
+release build, GCC 13.3), `bench_bitcoin -filter='VerifyScriptP2MR.*'` over 10,000 single checks
+per run, three runs per SHA-256 implementation of Core (generic and SSE4/AVX2; the vendored
+library always uses its own portable code):
+
+- a Schnorr check over its whole path took 48 to 75 us (median), an SLH-DSA check with real
+  signatures 622 to 677 us, and the worst case 1,041 to 1,170 us, through a measurement-only hook
+  that takes every WOTS+ message digit as zero (3,894 SHA-256 compressions per check against a
+  measured mean of 2,183 for real signatures);
+- `1.25 x 50 x p99(worst case) / median(Schnorr)` came to 2,550 to 3,658 across the runs (each run's
+  bound rounded up). The p99 of that machine is about twice its median for Schnorr checks too,
+  which inflates the result.
+
+5,000 is at least 1.37 times the highest of these (3,658), a margin for hardware weaker than that
+machine.
+
+The measurement-only build (the worst-case hook, and a choice of Core's SHA-256 implementation in
+`bench_bitcoin`) is not part of this series: it is `contrib/m4-measure/` of the patch repository,
+with its scripts, among them a whole-block comparison on regtest (blocks that use up their weight
+on SLH-DSA checks, on Schnorr checks and on both, connected by a node without a signature cache).
+Configured with it, CMake builds `bench_bitcoin` and, for that script, a measurement-only node
+`bitcoind_m4_measure` that has no install rule, and nothing else: it refuses a configuration that
+defines any other executable or shared library target, `cmake --install` fails, and `bitcoind.cpp`
+and `qt/main.cpp` do not compile for any other target.
+
+Empty signatures use no weight. The budget of one input is shared by every check in it:
+`50 x N_Schnorr + 250 x N_ML-DSA-44 + C x N_SLH-DSA <= witness size + 50`.
+
+## Activation
+
+The rules are a buried deployment, `p2mr_slhdsa128s` in `getdeploymentinfo`. They require P2MR.
+
+- On the Ark-0 signet (challenge
+  `5121026fc5d8e79a9fbc8bc3dea07d82641d16717ce0b000c13c512d8e8c1788c6e5da51ae`) they apply from
+  `ARK0_SLHDSA128S_HEIGHT` in `src/kernel/chainparams.h`: **height 32000**, above the height of the
+  P2MR tripwire rules (30000), which a compile-time check enforces. The height is a rule of that
+  network, so there is no option to change it; once a build that carries it has been published,
+  the height and the validation weight do not change on that network. It covers this opcode only:
+  other rules of the network come with heights of their own. At the rate of about 870 blocks a day
+  that the signet had when the height was set, block 32000 is expected around 2026-10-25 00:30
+  UTC; even at the producer's maximum of 960 blocks a day it cannot come before 2026-10-24 04:00
+  UTC.
+- On another custom signet they apply from `-signetslhdsa128s=<height>`, which requires
+  `-signetchallenge` and which the Ark-0 signet refuses. A node refuses the option on any other
+  chain.
+- On the default signet, testnet3, testnet4 and mainnet they never activate.
+- On regtest they apply from genesis, or from `-testactivationheight=p2mr_slhdsa128s@<height>`.
+
+When the rules are enabled, the node logs
+`P2MR SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s rules active from height <height> (leaf versions 0xc0, 0xe0)` at startup;
+leaf version `0xe0` executes from its own height.
+
+The change is a soft fork. A node without it treats the byte as `OP_SUCCESS241`, accepts every
+block that a node with it accepts, and keeps following the chain; it only does not enforce the
+signature check.
+
+## Policy
+
+- **Before activation** the mempool refuses every spend that executes the opcode, even with a
+  valid signature, with `-acceptnonstdtxn` too: while the next block is below the activation
+  height the byte is still an `OP_SUCCESSx`, which `DISCOURAGE_OP_SUCCESS` makes non-standard.
+  From the block before the activation height on, the mempool checks such spends by the new
+  rules.
+- **Reorganizations**: when the chain goes back so that the next block is below the activation
+  height, transactions that execute the opcode in a P2MR leaf of version `0xc0` or `0xe0` are
+  removed from the mempool, together with their descendants, whatever the rest of the leaf.
+- **Template only**: in a leaf at depth one or more of version `0xc0` or `0xe0`, a script that
+  has `OP_CHECKSLHDSA128S` among its opcodes is standard only if it is SLH1, byte for byte. P2MR
+  leaves follow the tapscript limit of 80 bytes per witness element; the sixteen signature chunks
+  of SLH1, which must be the whole initial stack, are exempt from it, and only at their exact
+  sizes. Multisignature leaves, a leaf with both an ML-DSA-44 and an SLH-DSA check, and anything
+  before or after the template are not standard.
+
+## Wallets
+
+Bitcoin Core's wallet does not create or sign `OP_CHECKSLHDSA128S` leaves. A wallet that does
+should follow what [p2mr-mldsa44.md](p2mr-mldsa44.md) says for its templates: SLH1 next to an
+`OP_RETURN` leaf of the same leaf version, both at depth one; addresses only once the tip is at
+least six blocks past the activation height; funding transactions locked to the activation
+height; an output protected only when its tree is exactly that and it confirmed at or above the
+activation height.
+
+## Tests
+
+- `src/test/slhdsa128s_tests.cpp`: the verifier against the NIST ACVP SLH-DSA sigVer and sigGen
+  vectors of SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s, a signature made by @noble/post-quantum, other interfaces and
+  malformed signatures, and that no wrong length reaches the library.
+- `src/test/p2mr_slhdsa128s_tests.cpp`: the parameters and options of each network, the
+  signature messages against `src/test/data/p2mr_slhdsa128s_sighash.json` (written by
+  `test/util/generate_p2mr_slhdsa128s_sighash.py`), and the script rules, with spends signed in
+  the test by the vendored library: every rule above, each hash type, the chunk layout, empty
+  signatures, the validation weight, where the byte executes, the caches and the relay policy.
+- `src/test/p2mr_ts_m4_tests.cpp`: vectors made by the wallet side, p2mr-ts, written independently
+  (`src/test/data/p2mr_ts_m4_vectors.md`): their signature messages for key_version 0, 1 and 2,
+  their signatures, their witnesses through `VerifyScript`, their keys, leaves, trees, control
+  blocks, scriptPubKeys and addresses.
+- `test/functional/feature_p2mr_slhdsa128s.py`: activation, relay, reorganization and block-level
+  rejection on regtest; signatures by the test framework's own implementation
+  (`test/functional/test_framework/crypto/slhdsa128s.py`).
+- Fuzz targets `slhdsa128s_verify` and `p2mr_slhdsa128s`.
+- `bench_bitcoin -filter='Verify(SLHDSA128S|SchnorrBIP340)|VerifyScriptP2MR.*'` for the
+  measurements above.
